llama.cpp (as fetched by Ollama v0.35.1, llama.cpp b11232): one 16-byte load
for q5_K's scales in the decode dot product.

WHY
---
103 gives most K-quants one warp over two rows for single-token decode; q5_K
measured slower that way and keeps two warps per row, so its dot product
itself is what is left to speed up. vec_dot_q5_K_q8_1 reads the block's d/dmin
pair and three 16-bit scale words as four separate small loads. In block_q5_K
those 16 bytes are contiguous at offset 0 (half2 dm, then 12 bytes of
scales), so one 16-byte load brings all of them into registers and the three
scale words are picked from it by jm. The second sub-block's high-bit
extraction ((vh >> 1) << 4) & 0x10101010 equals (vh << 3) & 0x10101010,
because the mask keeps only bits that both shifts move from the same place;
the two sub-blocks are written out instead of looped.

Measured with this patch and 104 together (the two were not built
separately): test-backend-ops perf, MUL_MAT q5_K 4096x14336, 1/2/4/8
columns, 234.9/286.6/473.8/893.4 -> 224.6/278.2/471.4/852.4 us per run on an
RX 590. MUL_MAT 1301/1301 and MUL_MAT_ID 931/931 against the CPU backend.

The change is not gated on gfx803: it is the same arithmetic for every
architecture. block_q5_K is 176 bytes, a multiple of 16, so the load is
aligned whenever the weight data is, which ggml's device buffers guarantee.
Only gfx803 was measured.

WHAT
----
In ggml/src/ggml-cuda/vecdotq.cuh:
1. vec_dot_q5_K_q8_1 loads dm and scales as one int4 and takes s0, s2, s4
   from its .y, .z, .w words (low or high half by jm).
2. vec_dot_q5_K_q8_1_impl_vmmq computes the two sub-blocks without a loop,
   with (vh << 3) for the second one's high bits.
3. The block and activation pointers are __restrict__.

